['yes'] {'response': 'Yes, both Joseph Roth and Cid Corman were authors. Joseph Roth was an Austrian writer known for his journalistic work and literary contributions during the Weimar Republic, with notable works including "What I Saw: Reports from Berlin, 1920-1933." Cid Corman was an American poet, translator, and editor, recognized as a key figure in American poetry in the second half of the 20th century, particularly for his work with the publication "Origin." Their contributions to literature in their respective fields highlight their roles as authors.', 'query': 'Were both Joseph Roth and Cid Corman authors?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Cid (Sidney) Corman (June 29, 1924 – March 12, 2004) was an American poet, translator and editor, most notably of "Origin", who was a key figure in the history of American poetry in the second half of the 20th century.', 'node_id': '27e3b31f-c67c-4fa9-9ff1-330227226120', 'score': 0.8194947838783264}, {'text': 'Theodore Vernon Enslin (born March 25, 1925 – November 21, 2011) was an American poet associated with Cid Corman\'s "Origin" and press. He is widely regarded as one of the most musical of American avant-garde poets.', 'node_id': '90ab5b96-41bf-4da1-bcb1-791a2ef94acb', 'score': 1.0509424209594727}, {'text': 'In the 21st century, publications in English of "Radetzky March" and of collections of his journalism from Berlin and Paris created a revival of interest in Roth.', 'node_id': '5852e876-abd0-4e0b-b6d9-13794452f6d7', 'score': 1.052304983139038}, {'text': 'Meyer Levin (October 7, 1905 – July 9, 1981) was an American novelist. Perhaps best known for his work on the Leopold and Loeb case, Levin worked as a journalist (for the "Chicago Daily News" and, from 1933–39, as an editor for "Esquire").', 'node_id': 'a39095f8-287b-4785-ae76-faaed4066632', 'score': 1.0953218936920166}, {'text': 'Joseph Hillis Miller Jr. (born March 5, 1928) is an American literary critic who has been heavily influenced by—and who has heavily influenced—deconstruction.', 'node_id': '5b687bf6-31e5-4fdd-a8ea-97b4f9e970ed', 'score': 1.1113502979278564}, {'text': 'His work has been published in The New Yorker, The Paris Review, Slate, Poetry magazine, The Gettysburg Review, The Southern Review, and Five Points, among others, and he is the recipient of a Fulbright Fellowship in Poetry to Israel and a 2005 Guggenheim Fellowship in Poetry. He has also received, among others, a National Endowment for the Arts Fellowship in Poetry (1981), a New York Foundation for the Arts Fellowship in Poetry (1985), as well as the Levinson Prize from Poetry magazine.', 'node_id': '47504c1c-4281-4e24-8c44-47f9c189e611', 'score': 1.11580491065979}, {'text': "Jaroslav Kořán (January 17, 1940 – June 2, 2017) was a Czech translator, writer, screenwriter, and politician. A dissident and signatory of Charter 77 during Czechoslovakia's Communist era, Kořán translated over seven dozen books, mostly by American writers, from English into Czech, including major works by Kurt Vonnegut, Henry Miller, Roald Dahl, Ken Kesey, Charles Bukowski, John Kennedy Toole, and John Wyndham.", 'node_id': '97d4c899-1750-456a-bea3-b3f56393e28d', 'score': 1.132323980331421}, {'text': 'David John Connolly (born 1954) is an English-born Greek literary translator. He has translated poetry and novels from Greek to English, including writing by Nikiforos Vrettakos, Odysseas Elytis, Kiki Dimoula and Nikos Engonopoulos.', 'node_id': '23568fac-91c5-4f6a-be91-82cb88e1b7e0', 'score': 1.147939682006836}, {'text': "Gabriel Levin (Born 1948, Paris) is a poet, translator and essayist. He lives in Jerusalem with his wife and children. Levin is one of the founding editors of Ibis Editions, a small non-profit press devoted to the publication of the literature of the Levant, and serves as its Editor-at-large. Levin's work has appeared in numerous literary magazines, including PN Review, The Times Literary Supplement, the Chicago Review, Raritan and Parnassus.", 'node_id': 'c39ccc27-85a3-4c5d-ae7c-6e9c1be5a69e', 'score': 1.1594736576080322}, {'text': "What I Saw: Reports from Berlin, 1920-1933 is a book of reportage by the Austrian writer Joseph Roth from the era of the Weimar Republic. The selection of pieces from Roth's large journalistic output was made by Michael Bienert and published in German in 1996. The English translation with the present title was made by Michael Hofmann and appeared in 2003.", 'node_id': '85e4ada4-10af-4e86-9385-b40f5648bdd1', 'score': 1.1597154140472412}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.4618853889405727, 'vram_usage': 1.14208984375}
