  1%|          | 5/500 [01:21<2:42:16, 19.67s/it]  1%|          | 6/500 [01:25<1:58:35, 14.40s/it]['Sir C. V. Raman'] {'response': "The name of Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar's uncle, who is also a Nobel laureate, is Sir C. V. Raman. Sir C. V. Raman was a distinguished physicist and a member of the Chandrasekhar family, which is known for its contributions to science, particularly in the field of physics.", 'query': 'The Chandrasekhar limit was named after an astrophysicist, what is the name of his uncle of which is also a novel laureate?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'His mathematical treatment of stellar evolution yielded many of the best current theoretical models of the later evolutionary stages of massive stars and black holes. The Chandrasekhar limit is named after him.', 'node_id': '9f63f241-241d-41a3-9d0d-a0e2257e3168', 'score': 0.5275323390960693}, {'text': 'The Chandrasekhar limit ( ) is the maximum mass of a stable white dwarf star. The limit was first indicated in papers published by Wilhelm Anderson and E. C. Stoner, and was named after Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, the Indian astrophysicist who independently discovered and improved upon the accuracy of the calculation in 1930, at the age of 19, in India. This limit was initially ignored by the community of scientists because such a limit would logically require the existence of black holes, which were considered a scientific impossibility at the time.', 'node_id': '1c22f7fd-4de1-42cf-9916-ebb16a099c1a', 'score': 0.636629581451416}, {'text': 'The Chandrasekhar family is an Indian intellectual family. Several members of the family have achieved scientific distinction, notably in the field of physics. Two members of the family, Sir C. V. Raman and his nephew, Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, were Nobel laureates in physics.', 'node_id': '4c6154fb-8eee-4ec2-926f-0d073888b64f', 'score': 0.659711480140686}, {'text': 'Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, FRS (Tamil: சுப்பிரமணியன் சந்திரசேகர் ; ; 19 October 1910 – 21 August 1995), was an Indian American astrophysicist who was awarded the 1983 Nobel Prize for Physics with William A. Fowler "for his theoretical studies of the physical processes of importance to the structure and evolution of the stars".', 'node_id': '3cd247a6-904f-4b76-8661-155fb8aa1cce', 'score': 0.7196093201637268}, {'text': 'In astrophysics, the Chandrasekhar equation is a dimensionless form of the Poisson equation for the density distribution of a spherically symmetric isothermal gas sphere subjected to its own gravitaional force, named after the Indian American astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar. The equation reads', 'node_id': '2f5b3e45-6ae0-4cb6-a0e7-a0fdd9f1fe38', 'score': 0.9220234155654907}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.3445120193064213, 'vram_usage': 1.36474609375}
