 26%|██▌       | 131/500 [21:10<1:00:28,  9.83s/it]['President Richard Nixon'] {'response': 'Allie Goertz, known for her satirical songs, wrote a song about the character Milhouse from "The Simpsons." Milhouse\'s full name is Milhouse Mussolini van Houten, and he was named by Matt Groening after President Richard Nixon\'s middle name, "Mussolini." This naming choice reflects Groening\'s creative process, where he often drew inspiration from various cultural references. Therefore, the character Milhouse was named after President Nixon\'s middle name.', 'query': 'Musician and satirist Allie Goertz wrote a song about the "The Simpsons" character Milhouse, who Matt Groening named after who?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Allison Beth "Allie" Goertz (born March 2, 1991) is an American musician. Goertz is known for her satirical songs based on various pop culture topics. Her videos are posted on YouTube under the name of Cossbysweater. Subjects of her songs have included the film "The Room", the character Milhouse from the television show "The Simpsons", and the game Dungeons & Dragons. Her style has been compared to that of Bo Burnham.', 'node_id': '6a2192a3-a200-4f8b-9ff4-2ff62c44eab8', 'score': 0.7181921601295471}, {'text': 'Milhouse Mussolini van Houten is a fictional character featured in the animated television series "The Simpsons", voiced by Pamela Hayden, and created by Matt Groening who named the character after President Richard Nixon\'s middle name. Later in the series, it is revealed that Milhouse\'s middle name is "Mussolini."', 'node_id': 'c522e98a-7928-4142-bf8a-fe8ff82f0013', 'score': 0.8143730759620667}, {'text': 'Groening had been called to pitch a series of shorts based on his comic strip, "Life in Hell", but instead decided to create a new set of characters. While the rest of the characters were named after Groening\'s family members, Bart\'s name is an anagram of the word "brat". After appearing on "The Tracey Ullman Show" for three years, the Simpson family received its own series on Fox, which debuted December 17, 1989.', 'node_id': 'c9c9dc44-9b42-4735-9f2d-719e4fe6c4c0', 'score': 0.8201777935028076}, {'text': 'The book includes entire chapters devoted to key figures such as creator Matt Groening and James L. Brooks and Sam Simon, who helped develop the series. According to National Public Radio reviewer Linda Holmes, "Ortved\'s thesis, essentially, is that lots of people are responsible for the success of "The Simpsons", and their creator, Matt Groening, has too often been viewed as the sole source to the detriment of others who also deserve to be praised."', 'node_id': '66b26cc5-7313-4a0d-9442-a3ac04c231aa', 'score': 0.8240756988525391}, {'text': 'Groening had been called to pitch a series of shorts based on his comic strip "Life in Hell" but instead decided to create a new set of characters. He named the character after his father, Homer Groening. After appearing for three seasons on "The Tracey Ullman Show", the Simpson family got their own series on Fox that debuted December 17, 1989.', 'node_id': '899d2a3a-3794-4ba1-a817-89dc69aa091a', 'score': 0.8399577736854553}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.322867140173912, 'vram_usage': 1.38818359375}
