 26%|██▋       | 132/500 [21:26<1:11:23, 11.64s/it] 27%|██▋       | 133/500 [21:34<1:05:25, 10.70s/it]['Firs'] {'response': 'The genus Firs (Abies) has more species than the genus Chelone. Firs consist of 48–56 species of evergreen coniferous trees, while Chelone comprises only four species of perennial herbaceous plants. Therefore, the genus Firs has a significantly larger number of species compared to Chelone.', 'query': 'Which genus has more species, Fir or Chelone?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Firs (Abies) are a genus of 48–56 species of evergreen coniferous trees in the family Pinaceae. They are found through much of North and Central America, Europe, Asia, and North Africa, occurring in mountains over most of the range. Firs are most closely related to the genus "Cedrus" (cedar). Douglas firs are not true firs, being of the genus "Pseudotsuga".', 'node_id': '6a8e28e4-fdcf-4e6c-9a1f-f2909302e816', 'score': 0.9553825855255127}, {'text': 'Chelone is a genus of four species of perennial herbaceous plants native to eastern North America. They all have similarly shaped flowers (which led to the name turtlehead due to their resemblance to the head of a turtle), which vary in color from white to red, purple or pink. "C. cuthbertii", "C. glabra", and "C. lyonii" are diploid and "C. obliqua" is either tetraploid or hexaploid.', 'node_id': '2e77b210-904c-43b5-adcd-63dcc597992c', 'score': 0.9619510769844055}, {'text': 'Nineteenth-century botanists had problems in classifying Douglas-firs, due to the species\' similarity to various other conifers better known at the time; they have at times been classified in "Pinus", "Picea", "Abies", "Tsuga", and even "Sequoia". Because of their distinctive cones, Douglas-firs were finally placed in the new genus "Pseudotsuga" (meaning "false hemlock") by the French botanist Carrière in 1867. The genus name has also been hyphenated as "Pseudo-tsuga".', 'node_id': 'eadb0339-7e6d-49fa-91ef-9dd868854c9e', 'score': 1.0223873853683472}, {'text': 'Abies spectabilis (East Himalayan fir) is a conifer species in the family Pinaceae and the genus firs. It is sometimes held to include the Bhutan fir ("A. densa") as a variety. It is found in Afghanistan, China (Tibet), northern India, Nepal, and Pakistan. It is a large tree, up to 50 m tall.', 'node_id': 'feef4a26-7492-442c-a407-55428dd6a805', 'score': 1.032500982284546}, {'text': 'Callitris columellaris is a species of coniferous tree in the family Cupressaceae (cypress family), native to most of Australia. Common names include White Cypress-pine, Murray River Cypress-pine, and Northern Cypress-pine. "Callitris columellaris" has been naturalised in Hawaii and in southern Florida.', 'node_id': '79274a65-25a4-4925-8f27-346df412044e', 'score': 1.0937581062316895}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 1.0037560015916824, 'vram_usage': 1.15380859375}
