 44%|████▍     | 219/500 [39:34<56:18, 12.02s/it]['no'] {'response': "No, Pavel Urysohn and Leonid Levin were not known for the same type of work. Pavel Urysohn was a Soviet mathematician recognized for his contributions to dimension theory and topology, particularly for developing Urysohn's Metrization Theorem and Urysohn's Lemma. His work focused on mathematical concepts and theories. In contrast, Leonid Levin is a Soviet-American computer scientist, and his work is primarily in the field of computer science. While both individuals made significant contributions to their respective fields, their areas of expertise and the nature of their work differ substantially.", 'query': 'Were Pavel Urysohn and Leonid Levin known for the same type of work?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Leonid Anatolievich Levin ( ; Russian: Леони́д Анато́льевич Ле́вин ; Ukrainian: Леоні́д Анато́лійович Ле́він ; born November 2, 1948) is a Soviet-American computer scientist.', 'node_id': 'a08bbc51-3dee-4e3e-a539-f7af4f48e6b0', 'score': 0.7817530632019043}, {'text': "Pavel Samuilovich Urysohn (Па́вел Самуи́лович Урысо́н ) (February 3, 1898 – August 17, 1924) was a Soviet mathematician of Jewish origin who is best known for his contributions in dimension theory, and for developing Urysohn's Metrization Theorem and Urysohn's Lemma, both of which are fundamental results in topology.", 'node_id': 'f0ecbf8c-66cf-4d3c-bcef-1a3b93d5108e', 'score': 1.0745713710784912}, {'text': 'As a writer he is best known for the novella "The Remote House on Vasilyevsky Island" (Уединённый домик на Васильевском), which was influenced by the writings of Aleksandr Pushkin.', 'node_id': '61348308-41dd-4390-bccb-bde35f80f97f', 'score': 1.117100477218628}, {'text': 'Meyer Levin (October 7, 1905 – July 9, 1981) was an American novelist. Perhaps best known for his work on the Leopold and Loeb case, Levin worked as a journalist (for the "Chicago Daily News" and, from 1933–39, as an editor for "Esquire").', 'node_id': 'a39095f8-287b-4785-ae76-faaed4066632', 'score': 1.1297919750213623}, {'text': 'His name is also commemorated in the terms Urysohn universal space, Fréchet–Urysohn space, Menger–Urysohn dimension and Urysohn integral equation. He and Pavel Alexandrov formulated the modern definition of compactness in 1923.', 'node_id': 'e4d057ae-0e63-4f40-904d-27a565339918', 'score': 1.1966238021850586}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.7609678264707327, 'vram_usage': 1.22412109375}
