['Yarmouth, Maine'] {'response': "The globe that succeeded the Globe of Peace as the world's largest rotating globe is known as Eartha. It is located within the headquarters of the DeLorme mapping corporation in Yarmouth, Maine. Eartha surpassed the Globe of Peace in 1999 and has a diameter of over 41 feet (12.5 meters) and weighs approximately 5,600 pounds (2,500 kg). This transition marked a significant moment in the history of large globes, as the Globe of Peace had held the record for several years prior to Eartha's construction.", 'query': "Where is the globe that succeeded the Globe of Peace as the world's largest rotating globe located?", 'retrieved_context': [{'text': "The Globe of Peace (Mappamondo della Pace in Italian) is a very large globe located in Apecchio, Pesaro, Italy. It was the Guinness Book of World Records record holder for the world's largest rotating globe until 1999, when it was succeeded by Eartha. It was built over a period of six years by Orfeo Bartolucci with the stated goal of diffusing a message of peace and liberty to all people. It measures 10 metres in diameter and is reported to weigh 170 quintals, which probably translates to 17,000 kilograms.", 'node_id': '6c8b18c4-b252-40fe-b07e-68c546376c03', 'score': 0.28448641300201416}, {'text': "Eartha is the world's largest rotating and revolving globe, located within the headquarters of the DeLorme mapping corporation in Yarmouth, Maine. The globe weighs approximately 5,600 pounds (2,500 kg), and has a diameter of over 41 feet (12.5 m).", 'node_id': '7f80ff47-5d8d-47e2-b380-60e88f00b72c', 'score': 0.47724127769470215}, {'text': 'Arctic Circle is one of the five major circles of latitude that mark maps of the Earth.', 'node_id': '23402547-482a-4081-a561-4353f6979a94', 'score': 1.0953468084335327}, {'text': 'Reportedly 250 quintals of putty, 30 cubic metres of wood, and a ton (unclear whether English or Metric) of nails were used in its construction. The globe is located outdoors and has a fibreglass skin. It can hold approximately 600 people and internally contains descriptive tables listing every country of the world and their flag.', 'node_id': 'c3194d59-66a6-40e7-ae10-651031e3481f', 'score': 1.0953621864318848}, {'text': 'The Antarctic Circle is the most southerly of the five major circles of latitude that mark maps of the Earth. The region south of this circle is known as the Antarctic, and the zone immediately to the north is called the Southern Temperate Zone. South of the Antarctic Circle, the sun is above the horizon for 24 continuous hours at least once per year (and therefore visible at midnight) and (at least partially) below the horizon for 24 continuous hours at least once per year (and therefore not fully visible at noon); this is also true within the equivalent polar circle in the Northern Hemisphere, the Arctic Circle.', 'node_id': 'b8b568ee-6592-48fa-86ce-9c474ce162ef', 'score': 1.152662992477417}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'ENTAILMENT', 'response': 'CONTRADICTION'}, 'latency': 1.3265339694917202, 'vram_usage': 1.25927734375}
