  3%|▎         | 15/500 [02:11<1:29:53, 11.12s/it]['Donald Hugh Henley'] {'response': '"The End of the Innocence" was a single by Don Henley, who is a former member of the Eagles. Henley is known as a founding member of the Eagles and was the drummer and co-lead singer for the band from 1971 to 1980, and again from 1994 to 2016. The song was released in 1989 as the lead single and title track from his third solo studio album of the same name.', 'query': 'The End of the Innocence was a single by which former member of the Eagles?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': '"The End of the Innocence" is the lead single and title track from Don Henley\'s third solo studio album of the same name, released in 1989. Henley co-wrote and co-produced the song with Bruce Hornsby, who also performed piano; both artists perform the song live in their respective concerts. Henley\'s version peaked at number eight on the "Billboard" Hot 100, becoming his fifth solo top ten hit on the chart, more than any of the other members of The Eagles.', 'node_id': 'dbce5a20-be99-4d18-8463-4ec3ae04b88f', 'score': 0.5553935170173645}, {'text': '"The End of the Innocence" also became his fourth number-one single on the Mainstream Rock Tracks chart and peaked at number two on the Hot Adult Contemporary chart. The song features Wayne Shorter on saxophone.', 'node_id': '1372daef-7f03-4af4-b49d-d154e90c2519', 'score': 0.8591404557228088}, {'text': 'Donald Hugh Henley (born July 22, 1947) is an American singer-songwriter, producer, and drummer, best known as a founding member of the Eagles before launching a successful solo career. Henley was the drummer and co-lead singer for the Eagles from 1971 to 1980, when the band broke up, and from 1994 to 2016, when they reunited.', 'node_id': '17735359-df76-4713-a046-8f483c8a153c', 'score': 0.9746325612068176}, {'text': 'Following a year-long break due to Eagles founder Glenn Frey\'s death, Henley reformed the band in summer 2017 for the Classic West and Classic East rock festivals, hiring Vince Gill and Deacon Frey to replace Glenn. Henley sang the lead vocals on Eagles hits such as "Witchy Woman", "Desperado", "Best of My Love", "One of These Nights", "Hotel California", "Life in the Fast Lane", "The Long Run" and "Get Over It".', 'node_id': '77100489-76e0-46c6-a09b-10923eeef8d5', 'score': 1.1271289587020874}, {'text': 'His film appearances included "Where Eagles Dare" (1968), "Patton" (1970), "International Velvet" (1978) and "Champions" (1984).', 'node_id': '8553fa41-d8fe-46b9-a161-45ecde858ac8', 'score': 1.2588353157043457}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'ENTAILMENT', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.1516114734113216, 'vram_usage': 1.21240234375}
