\'s family members, Bart\'s name is an anagram of the word "brat". After appearing on "The Tracey Ullman Show" for three years, the Simpson family received its own series on Fox, which debuted December 17, 1989.', 'node_id': 'c9c9dc44-9b42-4735-9f2d-719e4fe6c4c0', 'score': 0.8201777935028076}, {'text': 'The book includes entire chapters devoted to key figures such as creator Matt Groening and James L. Brooks and Sam Simon, who helped develop the series. According to National Public Radio reviewer Linda Holmes, "Ortved\'s thesis, essentially, is that lots of people are responsible for the success of "The Simpsons", and their creator, Matt Groening, has too often been viewed as the sole source to the detriment of others who also deserve to be praised."', 'node_id': '66b26cc5-7313-4a0d-9442-a3ac04c231aa', 'score': 0.8240756988525391}, {'text': 'Groening had been called to pitch a series of shorts based on his comic strip "Life in Hell" but instead decided to create a new set of characters. He named the character after his father, Homer Groening. After appearing for three seasons on "The Tracey Ullman Show", the Simpson family got their own series on Fox that debuted December 17, 1989.', 'node_id': '899d2a3a-3794-4ba1-a817-89dc69aa091a', 'score': 0.8399577736854553}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.3831489719450474, 'vram_usage': 1.38818359375}
