 53%|█████▎    | 266/500 [45:04<39:22, 10.10s/it] 53%|█████▎    | 267/500 [45:08<32:12,  8.29s/it] 54%|█████▎    | 268/500 [45:14<28:30,  7.37s/it]['I Will Always Love You'] {'response': 'The re-recording featured on "Heartbreak Express," Dolly Parton\'s 24th solo studio album, is "I Will Always Love You." This song was originally recorded in 1973 and was included in the "Best Little Whorehouse in Texas" film soundtrack, which was released in July 1982. The reworking of "I Will Always Love You" topped the U.S. country charts in September 1982.', 'query': 'Heartbreak Express, Dolly Parton\'s 24th solo studio album, featured this re-recording of what song, originally recorded in 1973 and featured in the "Best Little Whorehouse in Texas Soundtrack"?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': '"Heartbreak Express" is a song written and performed by American entertainer Dolly Parton. It was released in May 1982 as the second single and title track from her album "Heartbreak Express". The song peaked at number 7 on the U.S. country chart.', 'node_id': '966a44d5-29e5-4669-9a8e-38b76811f712', 'score': 0.5811364650726318}, {'text': 'Heartbreak Express is Dolly Parton\'s 24th solo studio album. Released in April 1982, the album returned Parton to a more fully realized country sound (a process she had begun on the previous year\'s "9 to 5 and Odd Jobs"), after her late 1970s pop recordings. The album\'s first single, "Single Women", a slow-tempo honkytonk ballad about a singles bar, was written by "Saturday Night Live" writer Michael O\'Donoghue, and had previously appeared in an "SNL" skit in late 1980.', 'node_id': 'a8a734b6-2fdc-41b3-ba89-89c690877c28', 'score': 0.5841100215911865}, {'text': 'The Best Little Whorehouse in Texas film soundtrack was released along with the film in July 1982. The album was produced by Gregg Perry. In addition to the score of Carol Hall songs recorded for the stage soundtrack, the film soundtrack included two Dolly Parton compositions: "Sneakin\' Around", which she performed as a duet with costar Burt Reynolds and a reworking of her 1974 classic "I Will Always Love You", the latter of which topped the U.S. country charts in September 1982.', 'node_id': '7602329f-d931-417e-86fe-5c323a4d1d1a', 'score': 0.6463609933853149}, {'text': 'Dolly Parton\'s cover of the 1965 Petula Clark hit "Downtown" was the album\'s second single. The title song was originally a hit for The Platters in 1956.', 'node_id': '445f1738-a8f5-416a-88bc-d8cdfd98fb27', 'score': 0.9589622020721436}, {'text': 'Burlap & Satin is the now out-of-print 25th solo studio album by Dolly Parton. Released on June 18, 1983, it straddled the line between pop and country sounds. Consisting mostly of Parton\'s own compositions, two tracks were outtakes from the "Best Little Whorehouse in Texas" film: "A Cowboy\'s Ways" (a song intended for costar Burt Reynolds to perform in the film, that was ultimately cut out of the film) and "A Gamble Either Way".', 'node_id': 'e3541436-b6e3-4ad8-b4a9-38f193546893', 'score': 0.9856377243995667}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'CONTRADICTION'}, 'latency': 1.221214460209012, 'vram_usage': 1.49365234375}
