testified against his former associates.', 'node_id': 'cc39ce16-5d69-44d9-b50d-240e9f6fbfd5', 'score': 0.7538853883743286}, {'text': 'Fred H. Moore was a socialist lawyer and the defense attorney of the controversial Sacco and Vanzetti case. He had collaborated in many labor and Industrial Workers of the World trials. He played a minor role in several celebrated I.W.W. trials, including the Los Angeles Times bombing case in 1911 and the trial of Ettor-Giovannitti case, which arose from the 1912 Lawrence, Massachusetts, textile strike. Following acquittal in the Ettor-Giovannitti case, Moore spent the next several years roaming the country defending I.W.W. organizers.', 'node_id': '4c2ff2b3-eb3e-4cb9-a63f-64867aa763db', 'score': 1.0085773468017578}, {'text': 'Richard Leonard Kuklinski (April 11, 1935 – March 5, 2006) was an American contract killer and serial killer who was convicted of murdering five people, though he likely killed at least several dozen more. He was associated with members of the American Mafia, namely the DeCavalcante crime family of Newark, New Jersey, and the Five Families of New York City.', 'node_id': '8c14013d-16f4-4be1-a803-d4c807d6bddf', 'score': 1.0193930864334106}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 1.194571467116475, 'vram_usage': 1.22412109375}
