['Dirt'] {'response': '"Hate to Feel" is the tenth track on Alice in Chains\' album "Dirt," which was released on September 29, 1992. The album "Dirt" peaked at number six on the Billboard 200 chart. It is notable for being the band\'s highest selling album to date, having been certified four-times platinum by the RIAA and selling five million copies worldwide.', 'query': "Hate to Feel is the tenth track on what Alice in Chains' album that peaked as number six on the Billboard 200?", 'retrieved_context': [{'text': '"Hate to Feel" is the tenth track on Alice in Chains\' album "Dirt" (1992). The song was included on the compilation albums "Music Bank" (1999) and "The Essential Alice in Chains". It has also been the ninth track on later prints of the album and eleventh on others. Some editions of "Dirt" may merge this song with the then-unlisted 43-second track "Iron Gland".', 'node_id': '10ba6083-9a5a-4684-ab5c-23d19588d7ea', 'score': 0.47165125608444214}, {'text': 'Dirt is the second studio album by the American rock band Alice in Chains, released on September 29, 1992, through Columbia Records. Peaking at number six on the "Billboard" 200, the album was also well received by music critics. It has since been certified four-times platinum by the RIAA and gone on to sell five million copies worldwide, making "Dirt" the band\'s highest selling album to date. It is the band\'s last album recorded with all four original members, as bassist Mike Starr was terminated from the band in January 1993.', 'node_id': '3d99103a-9346-4ffe-9ca4-b01f06ebbcf8', 'score': 0.8647935390472412}, {'text': 'It peaked at number 6 on the U.S. "Billboard" Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart and at number 7 on the Canadian "RPM" Country Tracks chart.', 'node_id': '15c3df41-651a-4679-ac3f-69ce9cc05ec0', 'score': 1.007379412651062}, {'text': "The album debuted #1 on the ear X-tacy sales chart, #92 on the CIMS sales chart, #20 on R&R's specialty chart, and #7 on FMQB's Submodern Report.", 'node_id': 'ae9e46bc-ef90-4041-9404-cf8c2afd4376', 'score': 1.0459306240081787}, {'text': 'In the US, the track reached number 59 on the "Billboard" Hot 100 singles chart, becoming the band\'s second single to hit the chart after "There\'s No Other Way". It also reached number 4 on the Modern Rock songs chart.', 'node_id': '55c8d85b-6775-4842-bc23-72a252ffa8b7', 'score': 1.0482990741729736}, {'text': 'This also broke the record for the longest-length of time for a song to get to the top of that list after entering. The song reached number two on the "Billboard" Rock Songs chart. In one of the music videos of the song, the band is destroying an art gallery. On May 22, 2011, the song won Top Alternative Song in the 2011 "Billboard" Music Awards.', 'node_id': 'f61bf3cc-e344-42c1-a91a-c4176dab710b', 'score': 1.1014724969863892}, {'text': "Some critics, such as AllMusic, describe it as a one-hit wonder, owing to Quiet Riot's relative lack of critical and commercial success with following albums (and subsequent disintegration) towards the end of the 1980s. The title track was ranked No. 35 on VH1's 40 Greatest Metal Songs.", 'node_id': '46c0e815-7513-4581-a4a0-1ceffe1634b4', 'score': 1.1066899299621582}, {'text': "The album spawned only one single, the title track, which peaked at number twenty-four on the Hot 100, becoming Moore's only top-thirty song in the US and her highest peak to date. The song also reached number thirteen in Australia and was certified Gold by the ARIA.", 'node_id': '8db474b2-86e8-4da3-bfd9-0029c781698c', 'score': 1.1122006177902222}, {'text': 'The album also managed to peak at No. 6 on "Billboard"\' s Top Country Albums, making it the highest peaking album for the group on the chart.', 'node_id': 'd339daca-048c-4171-ab72-f60e2c92a90d', 'score': 1.1154539585113525}, {'text': 'A holiday album appeared before her platinum-selling fourth studio album "Thinkin\' About You" (1995). Reaching the third position of the country albums chart and the top thirty of the Billboard 200, its first two singles topped the Hot Country Singles chart. Her sixth studio album "Everybody Knows" (1996) spawned Yearwood\'s fourth number one single, "Believe Me Baby (I Lied)".', 'node_id': 'f13d01d2-81bc-480b-a26c-4374f6afa512', 'score': 1.1330593824386597}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 3.857236936688423, 'vram_usage': 1.30615234375}
